{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:02:25.346Z","ordinal":316603,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a1703b-4280-7320-9374-5c397c112b4d","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-55cad23d-9f16-4e5d-82bd-a709e792b37b","process_id":"94671","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","rg -n \"Nexus|folder trust|trust folder|approval mode|Review\" /home/mike/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -35 && sed -n '1,240p' /home/mike/.agents/skills/feature-engineering/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.0.0/README.md && sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n 'Nexus|folder trust|trust folder|approval mode|Review' /home/mike/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md","query":"Nexus|folder trust|trust folder|approval mode|Review","path":"MEMORY.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' /home/mike/.agents/skills/feature-engineering/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/home/mike/.agents/skills/feature-engineering/SKILL.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/v1.0.0/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"docs/v1.0.0/README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md","name":"CONCURRENCY.md","path":"docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"1:# Task Group: Nexus Code client release and Cloudflare contest publication\n2:scope: Package the installed Nexus Code Linux client and prepare the distinct Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission in `/home/mike/nexus-code`; retain separate readiness dispositions and do not treat CI or offline fixtures as a clean-account proof.\n15:## Task 2: Package Nexus Code as a complete Linux client with installer and CI, partial\n27:- for Cloudflare contest work, the user required using the existing repository, preserving unrelated history/work, and distinguishing the Nexus Code client from the Cloudflare Workers/Artifacts submission -> do not replace repositories or wholesale-integrate unaccepted candidates. [Task 1]\n34:- Contest-specific CI passed at `ce7e58b9ce3f6e4a72f84d205c5319b241ca0aa9` in workflow `38471712649`; it covered demo tests and Worker build/typecheck, not a real Cloudflare account run. The service is the submission; Nexus Code is its client/demo harness. [Task 1]\n45:# Task Group: Nexus agent effectiveness: unbounded continuation and evidence-bounded diagnosis\n46:scope: Review failed Nexus agent exports and maintain prompt/regression safeguards for progressing continuation loops, direct diagnostic questions, and timeout claims in `/home/mike/nexus-code`.\n57:- Nexus, nexus-session-session_20261014_091558_9m78.json, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, 54582+8192+4096>65536, make test, timeout, truncated output, direct status question, evidence-bounded diagnosis\n108:- Flash-Next Q-TOOLS passed 7/7 synthetic categories and 20/20 Nexus replay points. Q-LONGCTX passed probes from 16K through 59,913 tokens; a 67,335-token input was rejected with HTTP 400 in 0.14s. Both selected models passed production qualification after reboot and rollback restoration. [Task 1]\n146:# Task Group: Nexus dogfood-blockers independent candidate review and compaction evidence integrity\n147:scope: Review-only, isolated validation of `fix/dogfood-blockers` candidates against Nexus baseline; use before selecting an I1/objective-supervisor base or relying on context admission, unseen-result, or modified-file evidence.\n158:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 6b34f5f, 2860228, 6f71043, 6845415, unseen tool results, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, compaction, modified files, repeated tool-call IDs, editedPaths, editChangedFile, EALLOWSCRIPTS\n168:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 1b71e27, 2860228, MAX_READ_LINES, StreamRedactor, tester.ts, verifier baseline, BASELINE_FAILURES_ONLY, EALLOWSCRIPTS, native Git metadata\n172:- for review-only candidate work, the user required: “Do not modify Nexus source”, “use a scratch copy”, “If something is wrong, REPORT IT”, and preserve the operator’s dirty tree -> isolate builds/tests, preserve negative results, and report rather than remediate. [Task 1]\n192:# Task Group: Nexus architecture reconnaissance and context-engineering baseline\n193:scope: Strictly read-only mapping of Nexus information/context flow, durable state, admission, and validation; use when the requested deliverable is an architecture investigation rather than remediation or design work.\n196:## Task 1: Capture baseline for the intended A-I Nexus architecture reconnaissance, incomplete\n204:- Nexus, read-only, architecture reconnaissance, context-engineering, CURRENT-NEXUS-CONTEXT-ARCHITECTURE.md, 2860228, linear transcript resend, context admission, deterministic compaction, durable session state, no context router\n208:- for Nexus reconnaissance, the user required “read-only” and “do not modify source, tests, configuration, documentation, dependencies, Git state” -> preserve scope, record exact snapshot SHA plus concurrent-change risk, and do not implement optimization or prove a preferred theory. [Task 1]\n220:# Task Group: Nexus live Ratatui TUI review and redaction/observer isolation\n224:## Task 1: Review landed live Ratatui TUI changes on main, success\n240:- At reviewed HEAD `2860228`, Nexus had moved from a snapshot viewer to a live Ratatui client: multiline composer, approval overlays, slash commands, cancellation, tool-detail/evidence panels, receipt panels, and default TUI launch in a terminal. `--repl` remains the readline fallback. [Task 1]\n251:# Task Group: Nexus CRED-003 FlightRecorder and audit-log credential sanitization\n283:# Task Group: Nexus Cloudflare Artifacts capability, agent-native collaboration, and contest evidence\n287:## Task 1: Review Cloudflare Artifacts capability and documented platform limits, success\n327:## Task 5: Prepare the Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission, partial\n342:- for contest preparation, the user required the existing target repository and unrelated work/history to be preserved; distinguish Nexus Code (client/harness) from Nexus (the submitted service), and never present offline fixtures as real Cloudflare evidence. [Task 5]\n349:- Artifacts should own Git persistence, refs, objects, transport, isolation, forks, and repository token handling; Nexus/collaboration logic should own actor/task authority, intent, semantic contention, validation freshness, acceptance, and provenance. Textual overlap is not duplicate intent, clean merge is not semantic correctness, and ancestry is not authorization or acceptance. Prefer experiments with versioned work statements, exact candidate/evidence links, source/confidence/freshness relevance signals, state-change notifications, and explicit dispositions; avoid all-to-all broadcast reasoning. [Task 2]\n352:- Proposed minimum product: a Worker coordinates two isolated external Nexus runners, uses real Artifacts repositories/forks from one baseline, records exact-SHA validation/review, captures operator disposition, and integrates only the accepted candidate. Durable Objects fit serialized per-run coordination; external Nexus processes should perform shell/Git work rather than running the local agent in Workers. First implementation proof: create/import repository, issue a short-lived scoped token, clone/push a commit, then read that exact commit back. [Task 4]\n366:# Task Group: Nexus Phase 2 independent requirements and architecture review\n367:scope: Review-only assessment of `/home/mike/nexus-code` Phase 2 requirements, architecture principles, traceability, lifecycle semantics, authority boundaries, and Phase 2S carry-forward concerns; not an implementation or decomposition runbook.\n370:## Task 1: Independently review Nexus Phase 2 requirements, accepted with recorded concerns for Phase 2S\n378:- Nexus, issue-12, Phase-2, Phase-2S, ACCEPT_WITH_RECORDED_CONCERNS_FOR_PHASE_2S, CAP, AUTH, CTX, DATA, UI, EXEC-001, capability-identity, full-access, context-admission, sensitive-data, no-remote\n777:# Task Group: Nexus v1 CI prerequisite integration and release qualification handoff\n778:scope: Integrate reviewed CI prerequisites and accurately track Nexus v1 release gates, claims, and held-out boundaries; use for `v1/integration` readiness work, not as a substitute for exact-RC qualification.\n---\nname: Feature Engineering\ndescription: Create and transform features using encoding, scaling, polynomial features, and domain-specific transformations for improved model performance and interpretability\n---\n\n# Feature Engineering\n\n## Overview\n\nFeature engineering creates and transforms features to improve model performance, interpretability, and generalization through domain knowledge and mathematical transformations.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- When you need to improve model performance beyond using raw features\n- When dealing with categorical variables that need encoding for ML algorithms\n- When features have different scales and require normalization\n- When creating domain-specific features based on business knowledge\n- When handling skewed distributions or non-linear relationships\n- When preparing data for different types of ML algorithms with specific requirements\n\n## Engineering Techniques\n\n- **Encoding**: Converting categorical to numerical\n- **Scaling**: Normalizing feature ranges\n- **Polynomial Features**: Higher-order terms\n- **Interactions**: Combining features\n- **Domain-specific**: Business-relevant transformations\n- **Temporal**: Time-based features\n\n## Key Principles\n\n- Create features based on domain knowledge\n- Remove redundant features\n- Scale features appropriately\n- Handle categorical variables\n- Create meaningful interactions\n\n## Implementation with Python\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import (\n    StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler, PolynomialFeatures,\n    OneHotEncoder, OrdinalEncoder, LabelEncoder\n)\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Create sample dataset\nnp.random.seed(42)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'age': np.random.uniform(18, 80, 1000),\n    'income': np.random.uniform(20000, 150000, 1000),\n    'experience_years': np.random.uniform(0, 50, 1000),\n    'category': np.random.choice(['A', 'B', 'C'], 1000),\n    'city': np.random.choice(['NYC', 'LA', 'Chicago'], 1000),\n    'purchased': np.random.choice([0, 1], 1000),\n})\n\nprint(\"Original Data:\")\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.info())\n\n# 1. Categorical Encoding\n# One-Hot Encoding\nprint(\"\\n1. One-Hot Encoding:\")\ndf_ohe = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['category', 'city'], drop_first=True)\nprint(df_ohe.head())\n\n# Ordinal Encoding\nprint(\"\\n2. Ordinal Encoding:\")\nordinal_encoder = OrdinalEncoder()\ndf['category_ordinal'] = ordinal_encoder.fit_transform(df[['category']])\nprint(df[['category', 'category_ordinal']].head())\n\n# Label Encoding\nprint(\"\\n3. Label Encoding:\")\nle = LabelEncoder()\ndf['city_encoded'] = le.fit_transform(df['city'])\nprint(df[['city', 'city_encoded']].head())\n\n# 2. Feature Scaling\nprint(\"\\n4. Feature Scaling:\")\nX = df[['age', 'income', 'experience_years']].copy()\n\n# StandardScaler (mean=0, std=1)\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_standard = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# MinMaxScaler [0, 1]\nminmax_scaler = MinMaxScaler()\nX_minmax = minmax_scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# RobustScaler (resistant to outliers)\nrobust_scaler = RobustScaler()\nX_robust = robust_scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# Visualization\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 8))\n\naxes[0, 0].hist(X['age'], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[0, 0].set_title('Original Age')\n\naxes[0, 1].hist(X_standard[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[0, 1].set_title('StandardScaler Age')\n\naxes[1, 0].hist(X_minmax[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[1, 0].set_title('MinMaxScaler Age')\n\naxes[1, 1].hist(X_robust[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[1, 1].set_title('RobustScaler Age')\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# 3. Polynomial Features\nprint(\"\\n5. Polynomial Features:\")\nX_simple = df[['age']].copy()\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)\nX_poly = poly.fit_transform(X_simple)\nX_poly_df = pd.DataFrame(X_poly, columns=['age', 'age^2'])\nprint(X_poly_df.head())\n\n# Visualization\nplt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\nplt.scatter(df['age'], df['income'], alpha=0.5)\nplt.xlabel('Age')\nplt.ylabel('Income')\nplt.title('Age vs Income')\nplt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()\n\n# 4. Feature Interactions\nprint(\"\\n6. Feature Interactions:\")\ndf['age_income_interaction'] = df['age'] * df['income'] / 10000\ndf['age_experience_ratio'] = df['age'] / (df['experience_years'] + 1)\nprint(df[['age', 'income', 'age_income_interaction', 'age_experience_ratio']].head())\n\n# 5. Domain-specific Transformations\nprint(\"\\n7. Domain-specific Features:\")\ndf['age_group'] = pd.cut(df['age'], bins=[0, 30, 45, 60, 100],\n                          labels=['Young', 'Middle', 'Senior', 'Retired'])\ndf['income_level'] = pd.qcut(df['income'], q=3, labels=['Low', 'Medium', 'High'])\ndf['log_income'] = np.log1p(df['income'])\ndf['sqrt_experience'] = np.sqrt(df['experience_years'])\n\nprint(df[['age', 'age_group', 'income', 'income_level', 'log_income']].head())\n\n# 6. Temporal Features (if date data available)\nprint(\"\\n8. Temporal Features:\")\ndates = pd.date_range('2023-01-01', periods=len(df))\ndf['date'] = dates\ndf['year'] = df['date'].dt.year\ndf['month'] = df['date'].dt.month\ndf['day_of_week'] = df['date'].dt.dayofweek\ndf['quarter'] = df['date'].dt.quarter\ndf['is_weekend'] = df['date'].dt.dayofweek >= 5\n\nprint(df[['date', 'year', 'month', 'day_of_week', 'is_weekend']].head())\n\n# 7. Feature Standardization Pipeline\nprint(\"\\n9. Feature Engineering Pipeline:\")\n\n# Separate numerical and categorical features\nnumerical_features = ['age', 'income', 'experience_years']\ncategorical_features = ['category', 'city']\n\n# Create preprocessing pipeline\npreprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n    transformers=[\n        ('num', StandardScaler(), numerical_features),\n        ('cat', OneHotEncoder(drop='first'), categorical_features),\n    ]\n)\n\nX_processed = preprocessor.fit_transform(df[numerical_features + categorical_features])\nprint(f\"Processed shape: {X_processed.shape}\")\n\n# 8. Feature Statistics\nprint(\"\\n10. Feature Statistics:\")\nX_for_stats = df[numerical_features].copy()\nX_for_stats['category_A'] = (df['category'] == 'A').astype(int)\nX_for_stats['city_NYC'] = (df['city'] == 'NYC').astype(int)\n\nfeature_stats = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Feature': X_for_stats.columns,\n    'Mean': X_for_stats.mean(),\n    'Std': X_for_stats.std(),\n    'Min': X_for_stats.min(),\n    'Max': X_for_stats.max(),\n    'Skewness': X_for_stats.skew(),\n    'Kurtosis': X_for_stats.kurtosis(),\n})\n\nprint(feature_stats)\n\n# 9. Feature Correlations\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5))\n\nX_numeric = df[numerical_features].copy()\nX_numeric['purchased'] = df['purchased']\ncorr_matrix = X_numeric.corr()\n\nsns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', center=0, ax=axes[0])\naxes[0].set_title('Feature Correlation Matrix')\n\n# Distribution of engineered features\naxes[1].hist(df['age_income_interaction'], bins=30, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\naxes[1].set_title('Age-Income Interaction Distribution')\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Value')\naxes[1].set_ylabel('Frequency')\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# 10. Feature Binning / Discretization\nprint(\"\\n11. Feature Binning:\")\ndf['age_bin_equal'] = pd.cut(df['age'], bins=5)\ndf['age_bin_quantile'] = pd.qcut(df['age'], q=5)\ndf['income_bins'] = pd.cut(df['income'], bins=[0, 50000, 100000, 150000])\n\nprint(\"Equal Width Binning:\")\nprint(df['age_bin_equal'].value_counts().sort_index())\n\nprint(\"\\nEqual Frequency Binning:\")\nprint(df['age_bin_quantile'].value_counts().sort_index())\n\n# 11. Missing Value Creation and Handling\nprint(\"\\n12. Missing Value Imputation:\")\ndf_with_missing = df.copy()\nmissing_indices = np.random.choice(len(df), 50, replace=False)\ndf_with_missing.loc[missing_indices, 'age'] = np.nan\n\n# Mean imputation\nage_mean = df_with_missing['age'].mean()\ndf_with_missing['age_imputed_mean'] = df_with_missing['age'].fillna(age_mean)\n\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0: audit and planning package\n\nThis package is the evidence-based path from `v0.1.0-alpha.2` (audited at `db1ca55`, 2026-10-14) to v1.0.0.\n\n**Verdict: v1.0.0 readiness is BLOCKED.** There are 31 blocker requirements, none of them met. Task-quality, standalone and real-terminal qualification are **unverified**: no qualifying evidence exists yet. The contest entry was submitted, is frozen, and is tracked separately.\n\n## How to read it\n\n| If you want to… | Read |\n|---|---|\n| Understand what Nexus is today, what was examined, and what is wrong | [AUDIT.md](AUDIT.md) |\n| See every requirement (historical and new), its status, blocker class, and how it is accepted | [REQUIREMENTS.md](REQUIREMENTS.md) |\n| Know the order of work, the effort, and the critical path | [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) |\n| Implement a workstream | Its spec in [specs/](specs/), then its plan in [plans/](plans/) |\n| Qualify a release candidate | [ACCEPTANCE.md](ACCEPTANCE.md) |\n| Check what was actually run and what it proves | [EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md) and [evidence/](evidence/) |\n| See the decisions taken (all resolved under delegated owner authority) and the remaining owner actions | [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) |\n| Run several agents on this plan at once without collisions | [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) (also referenced from the root `AGENTS.md`) |\n\n## Workstreams\n\n| # | Workstream | Spec | Plan | Milestone | Blockers |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Safe execution defaults and approval integrity | [SPEC-01](specs/SPEC-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | [PLAN-01](plans/PLAN-01-safe-execution-defaults.md) | M1 | SEC-101, 102, 103, 119 |\n| 02 | Process ownership, cancellation, resource bounds | [SPEC-02](specs/SPEC-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | [PLAN-02](plans/PLAN-02-process-ownership-and-bounds.md) | M1 | AGT-109, SEC-106 |\n| 03 | Untrusted workspace inputs and egress boundary | [SPEC-03](specs/SPEC-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | [PLAN-03](plans/PLAN-03-untrusted-inputs-and-egress.md) | M1 | SEC-110, 111, 115, 116 |\n| 04 | Continuity, resume, durable work units | [SPEC-04](specs/SPEC-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | [PLAN-04](plans/PLAN-04-continuity-and-durable-work.md) | M3 | AGT-101, 102 |\n| 05 | Standalone local inference and provider robustness | [SPEC-05](specs/SPEC-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | [PLAN-05](plans/PLAN-05-standalone-local-inference.md) | M3/M5 | PRV-101, 102, 106 |\n| 06 | Packaged product parity, launcher, versioning, doctor | [SPEC-06](specs/SPEC-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | [PLAN-06](plans/PLAN-06-packaged-product-parity.md) | M0/M2 | REL-101, 102, 103 |\n| 07 | Supply chain, compliance, release engineering | [SPEC-07](specs/SPEC-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | [PLAN-07](plans/PLAN-07-supply-chain-and-release-engineering.md) | M2 | REL-107 |\n| 08 | Persistence hygiene | [SPEC-08](specs/SPEC-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | [PLAN-08](plans/PLAN-08-persistence-hygiene.md) | M0/M4 | (prerequisite: PER-103) |\n| 09 | Requirements governance and documentation truth | [SPEC-09](specs/SPEC-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | [PLAN-09](plans/PLAN-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | M0/M6 | OPS-101, 103 |\n| 10 | v1 qualification and evaluation | [SPEC-10](specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | [PLAN-10](plans/PLAN-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | M3/M5 | QUAL-101 to 107 |\n| 11 | Verification and tool-call integrity | [SPEC-11](specs/SPEC-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | [PLAN-11](plans/PLAN-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | M1/M3 | AGT-112, 113, 114 |\n| C1 | Contest (separate product; frozen until judging ends) | [SPEC-C1](specs/SPEC-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | [PLAN-C1](plans/PLAN-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | post-contest | — (not a client gate) |\n\n## How to execute\n\n0. If more than one agent (or person) will implement in parallel, read [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) first. It covers slice claims, worktrees, hot files, the gateway lock and merge order.\n1. **Start with M0** (ROADMAP). Merge PLAN-08 PR-8a first, so that test runs stop writing into the real `~/.nexus`. Then land PLAN-06 PR-6a (version sync) and PLAN-09 PR-9a (ledger checker, historical banners, cheap doc fixes).\n2. **M1 is the critical path.** Work through PLAN-01, PLAN-02, PLAN-03 and PLAN-11 PR-11a/b in the PR slices listed. Each plan names its safe change boundary, code locations, tests and rollback.\n3. **For every PR:**\n   - update the matching REQUIREMENTS rows (status, test reference, commit);\n   - keep `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` green;\n   - run `make test` on a snapshot copy, never while editing (tasks/lessons.md).\n4. M2 and M4 run in parallel with M1/M3. Held-out task authoring (agent-authored, as authorised) and the standalone proxy start during M1.\n5. Cut `v1.0.0-rc.1` only when G0–G4 pass. Then run ACCEPTANCE §4–§8 against the **installed** RC.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Requirement IDs.** `REQ-<AREA>-0xx` marks a commitment carried over from history. `REQ-<AREA>-1xx` marks a new v1 requirement.\n- **Finding IDs.** These are owned by the scoped evidence files: AGT, VER, PRV, SEC, PER, TUI, REL, DX, MNT, HIST, EVL, CON, PERF, DOC.\n- **Acceptance IDs.** `ACC-<AREA>-NN`. Each is defined in its spec, or in ACCEPTANCE §3.1.\n- **Evidence labels.** SRC, TEST, RT, MEAS, DOC, HIST, INF, UNV (REQUIREMENTS §1).\n- **Status ladder.** open → partial → implemented → tested → visually-accepted → product-qualified. Independent acceptance is recorded separately.\n\nThis package supersedes the Issue #12 matrices and the PHASE and WP documents as the source of current requirements. Those documents remain as historical record.\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n|---|---|\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n\n## 8. Independence constraints\n\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":26685}},"started_at_ms":1792890145345,"completed_at_ms":1792890145346}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:02:26.575Z","ordinal":316604,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a17094-8f0f-77e1-8d17-a19a2f63ca99","call_id":"call_a3ee476489054f9a929ad4edfce5de06","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 7960)\nTotal output lines: 448\n\n1:# Task Group: Nexus Code client release and Cloudflare contest publication\n2:scope: Package the installed Nexus Code Linux client and prepare the distinct Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission in `/home/mike/nexus-code`; retain separate readiness dispositions and do not treat CI or offline fixtures as a clean-account proof.\n15:## Task 2: Package Nexus Code as a complete Linux client with installer and CI, partial\n27:- for Cloudflare contest work, the user required using the existing repository, preserving unrelated history/work, and distinguishing the Nexus Code client from the Cloudflare Workers/Artifacts submission -> do not replace repositories or wholesale-integrate unaccepted candidates. [Task 1]\n34:- Contest-specific CI passed at `ce7e58b9ce3f6e4a72f84d205c5319b241ca0aa9` in workflow `38471712649`; it covered demo tests and Worker build/typecheck, not a real Cloudflare account run. The service is the submission; Nexus Code is its client/demo harness. [Task 1]\n45:# Task Group: Nexus agent effectiveness: unbounded continuation and evidence-bounded diagnosis\n46:scope: Review failed Nexus agent exports and maintain prompt/regression safeguards for progressing continuation loops, direct diagnostic questions, and timeout claims in `/home/mike/nexus-code`.\n57:- Nexus, nexus-session-session_20261014_091558_9m78.json, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, 54582+8192+4096>65536, make test, timeout, truncated output, direct status question, evidence-bounded diagnosis\n108:- Flash-Next Q-TOOLS passed 7/7 synthetic categories and 20/20 Nexus replay points. Q-LONGCTX passed probes from 16K through 59,913 tokens; a 67,335-token input was rejected with HTTP 400 in 0.14s. Both selected models passed production qualification after reboot and rollback restoration. [Task 1]\n146:# Task Group: Nexus dogfood-blockers independent candidate review and compaction evidence integrity\n147:scope: Review-only, isolated validation of `fix/dogfood-blockers` candidates against Nexus baseline; use before selecting an I1/objective-supervisor base or relying on context admission, unseen-result, or modified-file evidence.\n158:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 6b34f5f, 2860228, 6f71043, 6845415, unseen tool results, CONTEXT_CAPACITY_EXCEEDED, compaction, modified files, repeated tool-call IDs, editedPaths, editChangedFile, EALLOWSCRIPTS\n168:- Nexus, fix/dogfood-blockers, 1b71e27, 2860228, MAX_READ_LINES, StreamRedactor, tester.ts, verifier baseline, BASELINE_FAILURES_ONLY, EALLOWSCRIPTS, native Git metadata\n172:- for review-only candidate work, the user required: “Do not modify Nexus source”, “use a scratch copy”, “If something is wrong, REPORT IT”, and preserve the operator’s dirty tree -> isolate builds/tests, preserve negative results, and report rather than remediate. [Task 1]\n192:# Task Group: Nexus architecture reconnaissance and context-engineering baseline\n193:scope: Strictly read-only mapping of Nexus information/context flow, durable state, admission, and validation; use when the requested deliverable is an architecture investigation rather than remediation or design work.\n196:## Task 1: Capture baseline for the intended A-I Nexus architecture reconnaissance, incomplete\n204:- Nexus, read-only, architecture reconnaissance, context-engineering, CURRENT-NEXUS-CONTEXT-ARCHITECTURE.md, 2860228, linear transcript resend, context admission, deterministic compaction, durable session state, no context router\n208:- for Nexus reconnaissance, the user required “read-only” and “do not modify source, tests, configuration, documentation, dependencies, Git state” -> preserve scope, record exact snapshot SHA plus concurrent-change risk, and do not implement optimization or prove a preferred theory. [Task 1]\n220:# Task Group: Nexus live Ratatui TUI review and redaction/observer isolation\n224:## Task 1: Review landed live Ratatui TUI changes on main, success\n240:- At reviewed HEAD `2860228`, Nexus had moved from a snapshot viewer to a live Ratatui client: multiline composer, approval overlays, slash commands, cancellation, tool-detail/evidence panels, receipt panels, and default TUI launch in a terminal. `--repl` remains the readline fallback. [Task 1]\n251:# Task Group: Nexus CRED-003 FlightRecorder and audit-log credential sanitization\n283:# Task Group: Nexus Cloudflare Artifacts capability, agent-native collaboration, and contest evidence\n287:## Task 1: Review Cloudflare Artifacts capability and documented platform limits, success\n327:## Task 5: Prepare the Nexus Workers/Artifacts contest submission, partial\n342:- for contest preparation, the user required the existing target repository and unrelated work/history to be preserved; distinguish Nexus Code (client/harness) from Nexus (the submitted service), and never present offline fixtures as real Cloudflare evidence. [Task 5]\n349:- Artifacts should own Git persistence, refs, objects, transport, isolation, forks, and repository token handling; Nexus/collaboration logic should own actor/task authority, intent, semantic contention, validation freshness, acceptance, and provenance. Textual overlap is not duplicate intent, clean merge is not semantic correctness, and ancestry is not authorization or acceptance. Prefer experiments with versioned work statements, exact candidate/evidence links, source/confidence/freshness relevance signals, state-change notifications, and explicit dispositions; avoid all-to-all broadcast reasoning. [Task 2]\n352:- Proposed minimum product: a Worker coordinates two isolated external Nexus runners, uses real Artifacts repositories/forks from one baseline, records exact-SHA validation/review, captures operator disposition, and integrates only the accepted candidate. Durable Objects fit serialized per-run coordination; external Nexus processes should perform shell/Git work rather than running the local agent in Workers. First implementation proof: create/import repository, issue a short-lived scoped token, clone/push a commit, then read that exact commit back. [Task 4]\n366:# Task Group: Nexus Phase 2 independent requirements and architecture review\n367:scope: Review-only assessment of `/home/mike/nexus-code` Phase 2 requirements, architecture principles, traceability, lifecycle semantics, authority boundaries, and Phase 2S carry-forward concerns; not an implementation or decomposition runbook.\n370:## Task 1: Independently review Nexus Phase 2 requirements, accepted with recorded concerns for Phase 2S\n378:- Nexus, issue-12, Phase-2, Phase-2S, ACCEPT_WITH_RECORDED_CONCERNS_FOR_PHASE_2S, CAP, AUTH, CTX, DATA, UI, EXEC-001, capability-identity, full-access, context-admission, sensitive-data, no-remote\n777:# Task Group: Nexus v1 CI prerequisite integration and release qualification handoff\n778:scope: Integrate reviewed CI prerequisites and accurately track Nexus v1 release gates, claims, and held-out boundaries; use for `v1/integration` readiness work, not as a substitute for exact-RC qualification.\n---\nname: Feature Engineering\ndescription: Create and transform features using encoding, scaling, polynomial features, and domain-specific transformations for improved model performance and interpretability\n---\n\n# Feature Engineering\n\n## Overview\n\nFeature engineering creates and transforms features to improve model performance, interpretability, and generalization through domain knowledge and mathematical transformations.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- When you need to improve model performance beyond using raw features\n- When dealing with categorical variables that need encoding for ML algorithms\n- When features have different scales and require normalization\n- When creating domain-specific features based on business knowledge\n- When handling skewed distributions or non-linear relationships\n- When preparing data for different types of ML algorithms with specific requirements\n\n## Engineering Techniques\n\n- **Encoding**: Converting categorical to numerical\n- **Scaling**: Normalizing feature ranges\n- **Polynomial Features**: Higher-order terms\n- **Interactions**: Combining features\n- **Domain-specific**: Business-relevant transformations\n- **Temporal**: Time-based features\n\n## Key Principles\n\n- Create features based on domain knowledge\n- Remove redundant features\n- Scale features appropriately\n- Handle categorical variables\n- Create meaningful interactions\n\n## Implementation with Python\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import (\n    StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler, PolynomialFeatures,\n    OneHotEncoder, OrdinalEncoder, LabelEncoder\n)\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\nimport seaborn as sns\n\n# Create sample dataset\nnp.random.seed(42)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'age': np.random.uniform(18, 80, 1000),\n    'income': np.random.uniform(20000, 150000, 1000),\n    'experience_years': np.random.uniform(0, 50, 1000),\n    'category': np.random.choice(['A', 'B', 'C'], 1000),\n    'city': np.random.choice(['NYC', 'LA', 'Chicago'], 1000),\n    'purchased': np.random.choice([0, 1], 1000),\n})\n\nprint(\"Original Data:\")\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.info())\n\n# 1. Categorical Encoding\n# One-Hot Encoding\nprint(\"\\n1. One-Hot Encoding:\")\ndf_ohe = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['category', 'city'], drop_first=True)\nprint(df_ohe.head())\n\n# Ordinal Encoding\nprint(\"\\n2. Ordinal Encoding:\")\nordinal_encoder = OrdinalEncoder()\ndf['category_ordinal'] = ordinal_encoder.fit_transform(df[['category']])\nprint(df[['category', 'category_ordinal']].head())\n\n# Label Encoding\nprint(\"\\n3. Label Encoding:\")\nle = LabelEncoder()\ndf['city_encoded'] = le.fit_transform(df['city'])\nprint(df[['city', 'city_encoded']].head())\n\n# 2. Feature Scaling\nprint(\"\\n4. Feature Scaling:\")\nX = df[['age', 'income', 'experience_years']].copy()\n\n# StandardScaler (mean=0, std=1)\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_standard = scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# MinMaxScaler [0, 1]\nminmax_scaler = MinMaxScaler()\nX_minmax = minmax_scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# RobustScaler (resistant to outliers)\nrobust_scaler = RobustScaler()\nX_robust = robust_scaler.fit_transform(X)\n\n# Visualization\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(12, 8))\n\naxes[0, 0].hist(X['age'], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[0, 0].set_title('Original Age')\n\naxes[0, 1].hist(X_standard[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[0, 1].set_title('StandardScaler Age')\n\naxes[1, 0].hist(X_minmax[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[1, 0].set_title('MinMaxScaler Age')\n\naxes[1, 1].hist(X_robust[:, 0], bins=30, edgecolor='black')\naxes[1, 1].set_title('RobustScaler Age')\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# 3. Polynomial Features\nprint(\"\\n5. Polynomial Features:\")\nX_simple = df[['age']].copy()\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)\nX_poly = poly.fit_transform(X_simple)\nX_poly_df = pd.DataFrame(X_poly, columns=['age', 'age^2'])\nprint(X_poly_df.head())\n\n# Visualization\nplt.figure(figsize=(12, 5))\nplt.scatter(df['age'], df['income'], alpha=0.5)\nplt.xlabel('Age')\nplt.ylabel('Income')\nplt.title('Age vs Income')\nplt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()\n\n# 4. Feature Interactions\nprint(\"\\n6. Feature Interactions:\")\ndf['age_income_interaction'] = df['age'] * df['income'] / 10000\ndf['age_experience_ratio'] = df['age'] / (df['experience_years'] + 1)\nprint(df[['age', 'income', 'age_income_interaction', 'age_experience_ratio']].head())\n\n# 5. Domain-specific Transformations\nprint(\"\\n7. Domain-specific Features:\")\ndf['age_group'] = pd.cut(df['age'], bins=[0, 30, 45, 60, 100],\n                          labels=['Young', 'Middle', 'Senior', 'Retired'])\ndf['income_level'] = pd.qcut(df['income'], q=3, labels=['Low', 'Medium', 'High'])\ndf['log_income'] = np.log1p(df['income'])\ndf['sqrt_experience'] = np.sqrt(df['experience_years'])\n\nprint(df[['age', 'age_group', 'income', 'income_level', 'log_income']].head())\n\n# 6. Temporal Features (if date data available)\nprint(\"\\n8. Temporal Features:\")\ndates = pd.date_range('2023-01-01', periods=len(df))\ndf['date'] = dates\ndf['year'] = df['date'].dt.year\ndf['month'] = df['date'].dt.month\ndf['day_of_week'] = df['date'].dt.dayofweek\ndf['quarter'] = df['date'].dt.quarter\ndf['is_weekend'] = df['date'].dt.dayofweek >= 5\n\nprint(df[['date', 'year', 'month', 'day_of_week', 'is_weekend']].head())\n\n# 7. Feature Standardization Pipeline\nprint(\"\\n9. Feature Engineering Pipeline:\")\n\n# Separate numerical and categorical features\nnumerical_features = ['age', 'income', 'experience_years']\ncategorical_features = ['category', 'city']\n\n# Create preprocessing pipeline\npreprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n    transformers=[\n        ('num', StandardScaler(), numerical_features),\n        ('cat', OneHotEncoder(drop='first'), categorical_features),\n    ]\n)\n\nX_processed = preprocessor.fit_transform(df[numerical_features + categorical_features])\nprint(f\"Processed shape: {X_processed.shape}\")\n\n# 8. Feature Statistics\nprint(\"\\n10. Feature Statistics:\")\nX_for_stats = df[numerical_features].copy()\nX_for_stats['category_A'] = (df['category'] == 'A').astype(int)\nX_for_stats['city_NYC'] = (df['city'] == 'NYC').astype(int)\n\nfeature_stats = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Feature': X_for_stats.columns,\n    'Mean': X_for_stats.mean(),\n    'Std': X_for_stats.std(),\n    'Min': X_for_stats.min(),\n    'Max': X_for_stats.max(),\n    'Skewness': X_for_stats.skew(),\n    'Kurtosis': X_for_stats.kurtosis(),\n})\n\nprint(feature_stats)\n\n# 9. Feature Correlations\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5))\n\nX_numeric = df[numerical_features].copy()\nX_numeric['purchased'] = df['purchased']\ncorr_matrix = X_numeric.corr()\n\nsns.heatmap(corr_matrix, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', center=0, ax=axes[0])\naxes[0].set_title('Feature Correlation Matrix')\n\n# Distribution of engineered features\naxes[1].hist(df['age_income_interaction'], bins=30, edgecolor='black', alpha=0.7)\naxes[1].set_title('Age-Income Interaction Distribution')\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Value')\naxes[1].set_ylabel('Frequency')\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# 10. Feature Binning / Discretization\nprint(\"\\n1…960 tokens truncated…ents-governance-and-docs.md) | [PLAN-09](plans/PLAN-09-requirements-governance-and-docs.md) | M0/M6 | OPS-101, 103 |\n| 10 | v1 qualification and evaluation | [SPEC-10](specs/SPEC-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | [PLAN-10](plans/PLAN-10-v1-qualification-and-evaluation.md) | M3/M5 | QUAL-101 to 107 |\n| 11 | Verification and tool-call integrity | [SPEC-11](specs/SPEC-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | [PLAN-11](plans/PLAN-11-verification-and-tool-call-integrity.md) | M1/M3 | AGT-112, 113, 114 |\n| C1 | Contest (separate product; frozen until judging ends) | [SPEC-C1](specs/SPEC-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | [PLAN-C1](plans/PLAN-C1-contest-submission-track.md) | post-contest | — (not a client gate) |\n\n## How to execute\n\n0. If more than one agent (or person) will implement in parallel, read [CONCURRENCY.md](CONCURRENCY.md) first. It covers slice claims, worktrees, hot files, the gateway lock and merge order.\n1. **Start with M0** (ROADMAP). Merge PLAN-08 PR-8a first, so that test runs stop writing into the real `~/.nexus`. Then land PLAN-06 PR-6a (version sync) and PLAN-09 PR-9a (ledger checker, historical banners, cheap doc fixes).\n2. **M1 is the critical path.** Work through PLAN-01, PLAN-02, PLAN-03 and PLAN-11 PR-11a/b in the PR slices listed. Each plan names its safe change boundary, code locations, tests and rollback.\n3. **For every PR:**\n   - update the matching REQUIREMENTS rows (status, test reference, commit);\n   - keep `scripts/check_requirements.mjs` green;\n   - run `make test` on a snapshot copy, never while editing (tasks/lessons.md).\n4. M2 and M4 run in parallel with M1/M3. Held-out task authoring (agent-authored, as authorised) and the standalone proxy start during M1.\n5. Cut `v1.0.0-rc.1` only when G0–G4 pass. Then run ACCEPTANCE §4–§8 against the **installed** RC.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Requirement IDs.** `REQ-<AREA>-0xx` marks a commitment carried over from history. `REQ-<AREA>-1xx` marks a new v1 requirement.\n- **Finding IDs.** These are owned by the scoped evidence files: AGT, VER, PRV, SEC, PER, TUI, REL, DX, MNT, HIST, EVL, CON, PERF, DOC.\n- **Acceptance IDs.** `ACC-<AREA>-NN`. Each is defined in its spec, or in ACCEPTANCE §3.1.\n- **Evidence labels.** SRC, TEST, RT, MEAS, DOC, HIST, INF, UNV (REQUIREMENTS §1).\n- **Status ladder.** open → partial → implemented → tested → visually-accepted → product-qualified. Independent acceptance is recorded separately.\n\nThis package supersedes the Issue #12 matrices and the PHASE and WP documents as the source of current requirements. Those documents remain as historical record.\n# Running multiple agents on this plan without collisions\n\nThese rules apply to every agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Nexus itself, or a human) that implements any part of this package at the same time as another. The repository's root `AGENTS.md` points here. The rules come from incidents recorded in `tasks/lessons.md`:\n\n- a concurrent agent overwrote newer evidence;\n- full test suites were contaminated by edits made mid-run;\n- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n- HEAD moved during a pause.\n\nThey also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n\n## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n\n- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n\n## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n\nClaims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n\n```sh\nCLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\nmkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\nSLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\nif mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\nelse\n  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\nfi\n```\n\n- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n\n## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n\n| Resource | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n\n## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n\nThese files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n\n| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n| `cli/src/client.ts` | 03, 05, 08, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-03 PR-3a (env layering) lands before any PLAN-05 slice.** |\n| `cli/src/repl.ts` | 01, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n| `cli/src/config.ts`, `security.ts`, `sandbox.ts`, `mcp.ts`, `plugins.ts` | 01, 03 | Claim required. PLAN-01 PR-1e and PLAN-03 must not run at the same time on `sandbox.ts` or `security.ts`. |\n| `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts` | 01, 02 | **PLAN-01 PR-1a (effect classes) lands first.** PLAN-02 PR-2c adds schema fields afterwards. |\n| `cli/src/tester.ts` | 01, 11 | **PLAN-01 PR-1c lands before PLAN-11 PR-11b/11c.** |\n| `cli/src/core/state.ts` | 04, 08 | **PLAN-08 PR-8a (HOME isolation) lands before everything.** |\n| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n\n### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n\nA slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n\n## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n\n1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n\n## 6. Coordination records\n\n| Record | Where | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n\n## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n\nThe following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n\n| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n|---|---|\n| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n\n## 8. Independence constraints\n\n- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1703b-4280-7320-9374-5c397c112b4d","create_time":1792890146.5751472}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
