{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:25:42.230Z","ordinal":317853,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","session_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","root_turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","response_id":"resp_060b64278a9dfb15016add5a8ceb2481a08d36c0750c77cefc","usage":{"input_tokens":213790,"cached_input_tokens":213504,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1162,"reasoning_output_tokens":1145,"total_tokens":214952},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":23992913,"cached_input_tokens":23683200,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":76970,"reasoning_output_tokens":50093,"total_tokens":24069883},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":5775048848,"cached_input_tokens":5712832384,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16363750,"reasoning_output_tokens":9406047,"total_tokens":5791412598}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:25:43.340Z","ordinal":317854,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a170a9-df2b-72a3-ab8b-bba15993389d","call_id":"call_9b9cc5948ed747fead8a09f2e65142f0","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/current_main_rc_path_audit\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"**Disposition: keep REQ-SEC-101 and REQ-SEC-119 `partial` at c1f40e51b4d29b7110456b80e71984a31020eef3.** Their unit and integration tests cover important behavior, but the mapped acceptance criteria still require source-applicable PTY evidence before RC construction.\\n\\n- **REQ-SEC-101:** The policy, config, and headless-dispatch tests cover the review default and trust independence. They do not cover all of ACC-SAFE-01’s required combinations: both `nexus` and `nexus repl`, trusted and untrusted workspaces, review and auto modes, and the mock command.\\n- **REQ-SEC-119:** The verifier and harness tests cover review approval, script details, sentinel behavior, auto mode, and execution boundaries. ACC-SAFE-09 still requires PTY evidence for the approval panel; ACC-SAFE-10 requires `/test` cancellation within five seconds and a bwrap mount-info probe through PTY.\\n\\n`ACCEPTANCE.md` §1.1 makes source-applicable criteria pre-RC gates, and PLAN-01 calls for ACC-SAFE-01–10 to pass in CI. That is separate from the installed-RC manual terminal matrix. DEC-01 does not exempt either criterion. Next, obtain exact-revision evidence for ACC-SAFE-01 and ACC-SAFE-09/10, then review and update the ledger. No tests were run.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr207_independent_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"The repository does not explicitly promise isolation from a hostile process running as the same UID. `safe_fs.ts` describes ownership checks and descriptor verification, but not a separate same-UID security boundary. A same-UID process has the owner’s filesystem authority; a different UID is constrained by directory permissions.\\n\\nFor protection from different-UID actors, the smallest robust change is to verify ownership and write permissions across the parent directories before mutation, including `.nexus` and `.git/info`, and fail closed when another principal can rename those paths. Add a distinct-UID or equivalent permission-based adversarial fixture. The current descriptor checks still help reject symlink redirection.\\n\\nIf protection from hostile same-UID processes is required, this cannot be guaranteed with a final path recheck or ordinary Node `fs` calls. Node 22 exposes path-based opens and descriptor-based I/O, but no dirfd-relative `openat`/`openat2` API ([Node 22 filesystem docs](https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-jod/docs/api/fs.html)). A native Linux `openat2` helper can constrain path lookup, but it cannot stop a same-UID process from moving the opened directory afterward ([openat2(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/openat2.2.html)). That requires a real OS boundary, such as separate UIDs or constrained task execution.\\n\\nSo the post-open rename is a merge blocker only if PR #207 claims protection against arbitrary same-UID concurrent mutation. Otherwise, narrow the documentation and tests to the supported different-principal boundary and record same-UID relocation as a limitation. v1 supports Linux x86-64/glibc, so any native Linux mechanism would also need an explicit kernel requirement and fail-closed preflight.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","create_time":1792891543.3398912}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
