{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:32:02.447Z","ordinal":318129,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a170af-a80f-7f80-a8b6-0509e0108f1a","author":"/root/current_main_rc_path_audit","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/current_main_rc_path_audit\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED.** The canary is detectable from the host’s `/proc` across bwrap’s nested PID namespace in the intended Linux environment. But the fixture doesn’t prove the child started or that the scan can see it before Escape: it writes `started` immediately after `spawn()` and treats `/proc` read errors as no match. Either can produce a false pass. On failure, cleanup can also delete the fixture while a surviving child is still running.\n\nRequire a successful child `spawn` handshake and pre-cancel canary visibility; then check the canary is absent after Escape. Cleanup should terminate any leftover canary process after recording the failure. I ran no tests."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","create_time":1792891922.4473248}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":16041}}
