{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:40:17.128Z","ordinal":318467,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","session_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","root_turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","response_id":"resp_060b64278a9dfb15016add5e00262081a0ac1f59aa4f231fda","usage":{"input_tokens":141504,"cached_input_tokens":141184,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":42,"reasoning_output_tokens":25,"total_tokens":141546},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":35108854,"cached_input_tokens":34705920,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":106949,"reasoning_output_tokens":65398,"total_tokens":35215803},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":5786164789,"cached_input_tokens":5723855104,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16393729,"reasoning_output_tokens":9421352,"total_tokens":5802558518}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:40:17.967Z","ordinal":318468,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a170b7-37af-7260-a6e1-c48aedd9b272","call_id":"call_4f7e54ac9723432b9ca0820f8512c5ea","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/current_main_rc_path_audit\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr207_independent_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"The repository does not explicitly promise isolation from a hostile process running as the same UID. `safe_fs.ts` describes ownership checks and descriptor verification, but not a separate same-UID security boundary. A same-UID process has the owner’s filesystem authority; a different UID is constrained by directory permissions.\\n\\nFor protection from different-UID actors, the smallest robust change is to verify ownership and write permissions across the parent directories before mutation, including `.nexus` and `.git/info`, and fail closed when another principal can rename those paths. Add a distinct-UID or equivalent permission-based adversarial fixture. The current descriptor checks still help reject symlink redirection.\\n\\nIf protection from hostile same-UID processes is required, this cannot be guaranteed with a final path recheck or ordinary Node `fs` calls. Node 22 exposes path-based opens and descriptor-based I/O, but no dirfd-relative `openat`/`openat2` API ([Node 22 filesystem docs](https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-jod/docs/api/fs.html)). A native Linux `openat2` helper can constrain path lookup, but it cannot stop a same-UID process from moving the opened directory afterward ([openat2(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/openat2.2.html)). That requires a real OS boundary, such as separate UIDs or constrained task execution.\\n\\nSo the post-open rename is a merge blocker only if PR #207 claims protection against arbitrary same-UID concurrent mutation. Otherwise, narrow the documentation and tests to the supported different-principal boundary and record same-UID relocation as a limitation. v1 supports Linux x86-64/glibc, so any native Linux mechanism would also need an explicit kernel requirement and fail-closed preflight.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","create_time":1792892417.967059}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
