{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:53:09.144Z","ordinal":319146,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","session_id":"01a14cb3-25b0-7883-b3a1-0fa7cc559f92","root_turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","response_id":"resp_060b64278a9dfb15016add6103d69c81a09e278bc1d9b9100c","usage":{"input_tokens":100788,"cached_input_tokens":100096,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":108,"reasoning_output_tokens":91,"total_tokens":100896},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":49285126,"cached_input_tokens":48770816,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":137652,"reasoning_output_tokens":82913,"total_tokens":49422778},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":5800341061,"cached_input_tokens":5737920000,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":16424432,"reasoning_output_tokens":9438867,"total_tokens":5816765493}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T01:53:10.254Z","ordinal":319147,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a170c3-006e-7da1-8c95-7b74a9db9406","call_id":"call_5979df3daabf474e80b44f9a67fac513","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/current_main_rc_path_audit\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/pr207_independent_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"The repository does not explicitly promise isolation from a hostile process running as the same UID. `safe_fs.ts` describes ownership checks and descriptor verification, but not a separate same-UID security boundary. A same-UID process has the owner’s filesystem authority; a different UID is constrained by directory permissions.\\n\\nFor protection from different-UID actors, the smallest robust change is to verify ownership and write permissions across the parent directories before mutation, including `.nexus` and `.git/info`, and fail closed when another principal can rename those paths. Add a distinct-UID or equivalent permission-based adversarial fixture. The current descriptor checks still help reject symlink redirection.\\n\\nIf protection from hostile same-UID processes is required, this cannot be guaranteed with a final path recheck or ordinary Node `fs` calls. Node 22 exposes path-based opens and descriptor-based I/O, but no dirfd-relative `openat`/`openat2` API ([Node 22 filesystem docs](https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-jod/docs/api/fs.html)). A native Linux `openat2` helper can constrain path lookup, but it cannot stop a same-UID process from moving the opened directory afterward ([openat2(2)](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/openat2.2.html)). That requires a real OS boundary, such as separate UIDs or constrained task execution.\\n\\nSo the post-open rename is a merge blocker only if PR #207 claims protection against arbitrary same-UID concurrent mutation. Otherwise, narrow the documentation and tests to the supported different-principal boundary and record same-UID relocation as a limitation. v1 supports Linux x86-64/glibc, so any native Linux mechanism would also need an explicit kernel requirement and fail-closed preflight.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a17095-f2fe-7de0-b5cc-460661bc1fe3","create_time":1792893190.2548044}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
