{"timestamp":"2026-10-25T02:23:47.420Z","ordinal":320421,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a170df-08dc-7523-b2d4-7c766bb23301","author":"/root/pr207_independent_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/pr207_independent_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED**\n\nThe amended check correctly fails closed for a linked worktree when `FlightRecorder` is given the worktree root. It still misses a nested workspace: if `FlightRecorder` receives `<repo>/packages/app` while Git is unavailable, the code checks only for `.git` inside that directory. It finds none, returns, and allows flight state to be created there. See [git_exclude.ts](/home/mike/nexus-wt/git-exclude-fail-closed/cli/src/core/git_exclude.ts:128) and the marker check at [line 135](/home/mike/nexus-wt/git-exclude-fail-closed/cli/src/core/git_exclude.ts:135).\n\nWalk workspace ancestors for a `.git` marker when Git cannot resolve the exclude path, and fail closed if one is found. Add a nested-workspace regression test. The current nested-workspace test covers `WorktreeManager` with Git available, so it does not exercise this case.\n\nThe direct linked-worktree test is appropriate. Non-repository paths still no-op, and Git-available read-only exclude handling is unchanged by this amendment. I did not run tests; `git diff --check` was clean.\n\nReviewed effective candidate: `f2d7b7d03efbc3e6e3186a9d82c5c5fed2d44f14` plus two uncommitted file changes."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a170d0-7672-79e2-b11d-117e84b3ea44","create_time":1792895027.4201665}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":16132}}
